ok   root path matches
ok   dynamic segment is captured into route_params and params
ok   first matching route wins
ok   verb selects the route
ok   HEAD is answered by the GET route
ok   trailing slash and repeated slashes are ignored
ok   segments are percent-decoded, plus stays a plus
ok   a segment with a malformed percent-escape stays literal
ok   split_path drops empty segments and decodes
ok   split_path decodes escapes and multibyte text in one segment and keeps '+' a plus
ok   split_path: an invalid byte sequence in a decoded segment is QueryMalformed (400)
ok   the path segment policy: the whole path is validated, then a malformed escape stays literal
ok   the Router reads the request's cached path_segments; an invalid decoded segment is the 400
ok   a large non-ASCII path segment with an escape splits without a quadratic library call
ok   path_for builds paths from route names
ok   Route#path raises ArgumentError when a segment is missing
ok   Route#match returns captures or nil
ok   unmatched request gets a plain-text 404
ok   params assembly order is query < form < route
ok   ctx.params is the Router's own tree: built from the Request's validated texts, later sources win
ok   a request without a form body: ctx.params has the query and the capture, a later parse of the text is unaffected
ok   ctx.params never aliases the Request's path segments: mutating a capture in an action changes nothing Static reads
ok   later sources still win when each is parsed straight into ctx.params
ok   the Query limits apply per parse call: the query string and the form body each get MAX_PAIRS
ok   form body is ignored unless the request is a form
